# 6. Material Data Specification

Every material must contain the following structured information.

## 6.1 Basic Information

| Field | Description | Required |
|---|---|---|
| materialName | Material display name | Yes |
| materialCode | Unique internal material code | Yes |
| description | Product description | No |
| categoryId | Associated material category | Yes |
| brandName | Manufacturer or brand | No |
| productName | Actual product name | No |
| productModel | Product model or variant | No |
| productImage | Product image reference | No |
| technicalDocument | Product specification document reference | No |

The material code must be unique and generated or validated by the backend.

## 6.2 Material Type

The system should support material classifications such as:

- stain
- paint
- coating
- sealer
- primer
- cleaner
- surface_preparation
- repair_material
- other

Do not assume every material belongs to only one restoration process.

The category and material type should remain separate fields.

## 6.3 Surface Compatibility

Materials must define which deck surfaces they support.

Suggested surface values:

- wood
- composite
- other

For example:

Material: Exterior Wood Stain

Compatible surfaces:

- Wood

Material: Composite Deck Cleaner

Compatible surfaces:

- Composite

Compatibility must be explicitly configured by Admin using manufacturer specifications or approved business information.

Do not automatically assume that a material is suitable for every deck surface.

## 6.4 Product Specifications

Material specifications should support:

- Base type.
- Application method.
- Finish type.
- Color availability.
- Drying time.
- Number of recommended coats.
- Indoor/outdoor suitability.
- Weather resistance.
- UV resistance.
- Water resistance.
- Additional specifications.

Use structured fields where possible.

Avoid storing every specification in one unstructured string.

A flexible specifications object can support product-specific information without requiring a database schema change for every new specification.

## 6.5 Measurement Units

The material catalog must support appropriate measurement units.

Examples:

- gallon
- liter
- quart
- milliliter
- kilogram
- pound
- piece
- pack
- square_foot
- square_meter

The selected unit must be stored consistently.

The system must not silently convert between different units.

Any conversion logic required for estimating must be implemented and tested in Phase 6.

## 6.6 Coverage Information

Coverage represents the amount of surface area a material can cover using a defined quantity.

Example:

Material: Exterior Wood Stain

- Coverage: 200
- Coverage unit: sq. ft.
- Product quantity: 1
- Product quantity unit: gallon

Meaning:

One gallon covers approximately 200 sq. ft. under the specified product conditions.

Required fields:

- coverageValue
- coverageAreaUnit
- coverageQuantity
- coverageQuantityUnit
- coverageNotes

Coverage must be configurable because different products have different coverage rates.

Important:

- Do not hardcode 200 sq. ft. for every stain.
- Do not assume every material has coverage information.
- Some materials are measured per piece, pack, or other unit.
- Store manufacturer-provided coverage values accurately.
- Coverage must not automatically generate project quantities in Phase 5.

## 6.7 Material Cost Information

The catalog should support optional product cost information.

Fields:

- unitCost
- currency
- costUnit
- costNotes
- costLastUpdatedAt

Example:

Material: Exterior Wood Stain

- Unit cost: 45
- Currency: USD
- Cost unit: gallon

This is catalog reference information.

It is not the final customer price.

Important rules:

- Admin controls material cost information.
- Customer and Contractor cannot modify costs.
- Do not hardcode currency.
- Use the configured currency.
- Do not calculate final estimates in this phase.
- Do not add tax, labor, markup, discounts, or commissions.
- Keep cost fields optional when actual business data is unavailable.

Never invent actual brand prices, coverage rates, or product specifications.

Use clearly identified sample data for development and testing.

## 6.8 Material Status

Each material must support:

- active
- inactive
- archived

Rules:

- Active materials can be selected in future workflows.
- Inactive materials remain in historical records but cannot be selected for new work.
- Archived materials are retained for historical reference.
- Do not physically delete materials that are referenced by estimates or other business records.

